nodecode-approvals is a rewrite of anpicasso's anpicasso/hermes-jev-approvals:

  https://github.com/anpicasso/hermes-jev-approvals
  commit 28be98a19539b29768398fb400f4c62f41337c26
  MIT, Copyright (c) 2026 anpicasso (LICENSE)

The plugin registers TypeSafe's Jev decision model as the reviewer behind a
harness's smart-approval gate: one POST of a state and a fixed question set,
typed answers back, and a rule chain (jev_policy.py) that turns those answers
into APPROVE, DENY or ESCALATE. This rewrite keeps that: the six questions and
their rubric wording, the request and the answer check field for field, the
redaction passes, the endpoint and credential routes (TypeSafe direct, the
openrouter.ai route, or a base of the operator's own), the retries on 429, 529
and 5xx, the rule chain and its thresholds, and the local JSONL decision log.
It is Common Lisp against Nodecode's cell contract instead of a Python provider
plugin, so the caller is a Nodecode verb (`approvals:review`) rather than a
harness's auxiliary-model dispatch.

Left out: the harness's provider plumbing itself — the ProviderProfile, the
chat-completion duck type, the `auxiliary.approval` task routing and the
`plugins.entries` settings path — since a Nodecode cell is reached through eval
and keeps its settings in its own config section. Also left out: the
provider-side model listing and the benchmark harnesses. The audit log is
smaller: it records one row per decision, not the plugin's full 27-field
schema with policy and question fingerprints, and it is not rotated.
